<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Focal epilepsy with structural cause, after complete lesionectomy (G40.209)</content>. Seizure-free since the flight, three and a half months ago, and since surgery. After complete removal of a cavernous malformation with its hemosiderin rim, most patients become seizure-free; levetiracetam continues for at least two years before any taper is considered. [S]<list><item>Continue levetiracetam 500 mg twice daily and folic acid.</item><item>Graded return to training: running now, sprint sessions from six to eight weeks after surgery, full training by three to four months; no heavy lifting until eight weeks.</item><item>Swim only with a companion; keep the detection watch.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Cerebral cavernous malformation, completely excised (Q28.3)</content>. Pathology confirmed a cavernous malformation; postoperative MRI shows complete resection. No further imaging is needed unless new symptoms appear; resolved. [S]<list><item>Problem resolved.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Postoperative MRI</td><td>After resection of a cavernous malformation</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Day after surgery: complete resection, no hemorrhage</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Wound and neurological examination after craniotomy</td><td>After surgery</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Healed; examination normal</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Seizure diary and adherence</td><td>Each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No seizure; every dose taken</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Mood and behavior on levetiracetam (PHQ-9)</td><td>Before starting and at each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 4; irritability resolved</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Graded return to training</td><td>After surgery</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Written plan agreed with the neurosurgeon&apos;s limits</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Driving restriction and the California physician report</td><td>From the first seizure until cleared</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Not driving; review at six months</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Nocturnal seizure detection</td><td>When seizures may occur in sleep</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Watch worn nightly; no alerts</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Folic acid and contraception</td><td>Each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Folic acid daily; no pregnancy planned</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>EEG and MRI with susceptibility-weighted imaging within one week, and an epilepsy consultation within two weeks. Outcome by 1996-05-05: EEG in two days, MRI in six, consultation at two weeks [S]</item><item>No further seizures on an antiseizure medication she tolerates. In progress; target 1996-10-20 [S]</item><item>Neurosurgical evaluation of the cavernous malformation and a shared decision about lesionectomy within two months. Outcome by 1996-07-21: Seen by neurosurgery at three weeks; complete lesionectomy at eight weeks [S]</item><item>Safety plan in place: no driving until cleared, water and height safety, SUDEP discussion, and a nocturnal seizure detection plan. Outcome by 1996-05-27: Driving stopped and reported as California requires; SUDEP discussed (one visit late, recorded as a deviation); detection watch in use [S]</item><item>Graded return to full sprint training within four months of surgery. In progress; target 1996-10-20 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Krumholz A, et al. Evidence-based guideline: Management of an unprovoked first seizure in adults. Neurology 2015;84(16):1705-1713 (PMID 25901057) (2015); Akers A, et al. Synopsis of Guidelines for the Clinical Management of Cerebral Cavernous Malformations: Consensus Recommendations Based on Systematic Literature Review by the Angioma Alliance Scientific Advisory Board Clinical Experts Panel. Neurosurgery 2017;80(5):665-680 (PMID 28387823) (2017); Pack AM, et al. Teratogenesis, Perinatal, and Neurodevelopmental Outcomes After In Utero Exposure to Antiseizure Medication: Practice Guideline From the AAN, AES, and SMFM. Neurology 2024;102(11):e209279 (PMID 38748979) (2024); Beniczky S, et al. Automated seizure detection using wearable devices: A clinical practice guideline of the International League Against Epilepsy and the International Federation of Clinical Neurophysiology. Epilepsia 2021;62(3):632-646 (PMID 33666944) (2021); Governor&apos;s message returning Senate Bill 357 without signature (physician reporting of disorders characterized by lapses of consciousness remains mandatory under Health and Safety Code section 103900) (2024); Depression and Suicide Risk in Adults: Screening (2023)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Preventive visit next month. Six-month epilepsy review in October with the driving review. Call at once for any seizure, severe headache or new neurological symptom. Next scheduled visit 1996-08-25: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
